<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!-- Namespace is declared in build.gradle (AGP 7.3+); no package attribute
         here.

         ONE permission, and a host merging this plugin INHERITS it (#404):
         `registerNetworkCallback` requires ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE. It is a
         NORMAL-protection permission — granted at install, with no runtime
         prompt. (#407 R10d: an earlier draft here asserted it is ABSENT from the
         Play listing's user-facing permission set. That is not ours to promise —
         Play's presentation is Google's and it has historically surfaced this
         one as "view network connections". State what the OS guarantees, not
         what a storefront happens to render.) It reads connectivity STATE only:
         it grants no network access of
         its own (that is INTERNET, which any wallet host already declares) and
         no location. The plugin uses it for exactly one thing — learning that
         the device regained a network, so the sync loop can reset its retry
         backoff instead of leaving the badge on "Sync paused" for minutes after
         a FOREGROUND reconnect.

         A host that would rather not inherit it can strip it with
         `tools:node="remove"` and override `walletNetworkReachabilityProvider`
         in `zec_wallet_ui` with its own connectivity source instead. The wallet
         then degrades to the pre-#404 behaviour (the ladder runs its course, the
         sync sheet's "Try now" still works); nothing breaks.

         Otherwise this plugin still adds no activities and no services: it
         toggles FLAG_SECURE on the host's own Activity window via ActivityAware,
         and registers one process-scoped network callback. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />
</manifest>
